    reviewCommentary: 'Point Counter Point is Huxley\'s most ambitious novel, a work of ideas that weaves multiple storylines in the structure of a musical counterpoint. The novel\'s characters, including a scientist, a politician, a novelist, and a mystic, represent different philosophical positions, and their interactions explore every major idea of the 1920s. The novel\'s most famous character, Mark Rampion, is based on D.H. Lawrence, and its most controversial, Maurice Spandrell, is a terrorist intellectual. It is the most intellectual novel of the modernist era.',
