    reviewCommentary: 'Island is Huxley\'s final novel, a utopian response to Brave New World. The novel\'s fictional island of Pala, where Buddhism and science coexist and where children are raised with sexual freedom and ecological awareness, is Huxley\'s vision of a good society. Will Farnaby, the cynical journalist who is shipwrecked on the island, is the reader\'s surrogate, and his conversion to the Palanese way of life is the novel\'s plot. The novel\'s famous mynah birds, which cry Attention, are one of literature\'s most charming devices.',
