    reviewCommentary: 'Lady Chatterley\'s Lover is Lawrence\'s most famous and most controversial novel, the book that was banned in England until 1960. The affair between Constance Chatterley, the wife of a paralyzed aristocrat, and Oliver Mellors, the gamekeeper, is the most explicit love story in serious fiction. Lawrence\'s use of four-letter words, which was the reason for the ban, is inseparable from his argument that sex is sacred and that industrial society has alienated us from our bodies. The trial that lifted the ban is one of the landmarks of literary freedom.',
