    reviewCommentary: 'The Age of Anxiety is Auden\'s longest and most ambitious poem, a baroque eclogue set in a New York bar during the war. The four strangers, Quant, Malin, Rosetta, and Emble, represent different aspects of modern anxiety, and their conversations and dreams explore the spiritual crisis of the twentieth century. The poem\'s famous opening, in a bar, gives it the most urban setting of any major poem. Auden won the Pulitzer for this poem, and its title became a catchphrase for the postwar era.',
