    reviewCommentary: 'Enemies of Promise is the most honest book about the writing life ever written, a combination of criticism and autobiography that asks why most writers fail. Connolly\'s famous opening, no book is worth reading that is not worth reading twice, is one of the most quoted lines in literary criticism. His analysis of the enemies of promise, from journalism to domesticity to politics, is both funny and devastating. The book\'s final section, a portrait of his own childhood, is one of the most beautiful memoirs in English.',
